(* README *)

1 - Summary
    -------

    M001 formalizes constructive regulator theory as a syntactic,
    checker-first proof system for closed implication/falsity formulas.

    The object language is deliberately small:

      A, B ::= Bot | Imp A B

    There are no object-level atoms, no object-level substitution operation,
    and no semantic truth predicate. M001 is a regulator-theory substrate: it
    defines formulas, contexts, axiom sets, proof scripts, Boolean checking,
    checked derivability, inductive derivability, symbolic regulators, and
    evaluation-closure interfaces.

    The stable public import is:

      From M001 Require Import M001_95_API.

    The package is constructive. It provides deduction, constructive
    negation introduction, modus-ponens composition, syntactic adequacy,
    negative precomposition, and symbolic-regulator adequacy. It does not
    provide arithmetic provability predicates, modal provability machinery,
    semantic validity, external model theory, or diagonal self-recognition.
    L001 is the first downstream fixed-point and obstruction layer over this
    substrate.


2 - Core Objects
    -------------

    M001_00_Premises.v registers the base syntactic and regulator objects.
    These are the conceptual primitives for the whole package:

      Formula
      Context
      AxiomSet
      FiniteAxiomSet
      RegulatorLogicProfile
      RegulatorTheory
      BooleanEnvironment
      Justification
      ProofLine
      Proof

    `AxiomSet` is the abstract axiom predicate boundary:

      axiom_set_contains_bool : Formula -> bool

    `FiniteAxiomSet` is the finite data boundary:

      finite_axiom_set_formulas : list Formula

    `RegulatorTheory` is the minimal wrapper around a logic profile and an
    axiom set:

      regulator_theory_profile   : RegulatorLogicProfile
      regulator_theory_axiom_set : AxiomSet

    `BooleanEnvironment` is a small bookkeeping environment for data that is
    explicitly represented by Boolean predicates. In M001 it is used to
    register regulator theories as Boolean-operational objects, not to add
    model-theoretic semantics.


3 - Checker
    -------

    Proof scripts are finite lists of proof lines. A line is justified as an
    assumption, an available axiom, or modus ponens from earlier lines:

      J_Assumption
      J_Axiom
      J_MP i j

    The Boolean checker validates a proof script against a regulator theory,
    context, and requested conclusion:

      regulator_theory_check_bool R Gamma p A = true

    The checker is total and syntactic. It rejects empty scripts, checks that
    modus-ponens references point into the already-checked prefix, and accepts
    both modus-ponens orientations at the certificate level. The unordered
    modus-ponens convention is only a certificate convenience; the closure
    principle remains ordinary modus ponens.

    Finite axiom sets are connected to regulator theories by:

      finite_axiom_set_to_axiom_set
      finite_axiom_set_to_regulator_theory
      finite_axiom_set_check_bool


4 - Constructive Proof Operations
    -----------------------------

    M001_02__Abstraction.v proves the checked deduction theorem by an
    executable proof transformer:

      regulator_theory_deduction_transform A p

    The theorem is:

      regulator_theory_deduction_checked :
        regulator_theory_check_bool R (ctx_extend A Gamma) p B = true ->
        regulator_theory_check_bool R Gamma
          (regulator_theory_deduction_transform A p)
          (Imp A B) = true

    M001_03__Falsity.v defines object-language negation as an abbreviation:

      formula_negation A = Imp A Bot

    Constructive reductio is deduction at bottom:

      regulator_theory_reductio_transform A p =
        regulator_theory_deduction_transform A p

    The constructive theorem is:

      regulator_theory_reductio_checked :
        regulator_theory_check_bool R (ctx_extend A Gamma) p Bot = true ->
        regulator_theory_check_bool R Gamma
          (regulator_theory_reductio_transform A p)
          (formula_negation A) = true

    This is not classical reductio and not double-negation elimination.
    The same file also exposes computed, paired, checked, and raw finite
    reductio certificate shapes.

    M001_05__Application.v proves checked proof-script composition for modus
    ponens:

      regulator_theory_mp_compose_checked_lemma :
        regulator_theory_check_bool R Gamma p_imp (Imp A B) = true ->
        regulator_theory_check_bool R Gamma p_arg A = true ->
        regulator_theory_check_bool R Gamma
          (regulator_theory_mp_compose B p_imp p_arg)
          B = true

    At the existential level this becomes:

      regulator_theory_checked_derivable_mp_lemma

    The same file also provides one-line checked-derivability witnesses for
    assumptions and available axioms.


5 - Derivability and Adequacy
    -------------------------

    M001_04__Certificates.v introduces the checked derivability vocabulary:

      regulator_theory_proof_certificate
      regulator_theory_checked_derivable
      regulator_theory_not_checked_derivable
      finite_axiom_set_checked_derivable
      finite_axiom_set_not_checked_derivable

    M001_06__Syntactic_Adequacy.v defines the inductive closure:

      regulator_theory_inductive_derivable

    The notation `⊢ᵢ` in comments denotes this inductive closure only. The
    file then proves the central adequacy theorem:

      regulator_theory_checked_derivable_iff_inductive_derivable_lemma

    Thus Boolean proof checking and the inductive closure generated by
    assumptions, axioms, and modus ponens coincide.

    The exported induction principle is:

      regulator_theory_checked_derivable_induction


6 - Negative Transport and Monotonicity
    -----------------------------------

    M001_07__Obstruction.v proves negative precomposition:

      regulator_theory_not_checked_derivable_precompose_lemma :
        regulator_theory_checked_derivable R Gamma (Imp A B) ->
        regulator_theory_not_checked_derivable R Gamma B ->
        regulator_theory_not_checked_derivable R Gamma A

    This theorem transports a supplied non-derivability claim backward
    through a closed implication. It does not prove non-derivability of any
    particular formula by itself.

    The same file provides context and theory monotonicity:

      context_included
      axiom_set_included
      regulator_theory_included
      finite_axiom_set_included

    together with checked-derivability monotonicity and non-derivability
    antitonicity lemmas.


7 - Symbolic Regulator
    ------------------

    M001_08__Symbolic_Regulator.v presents the native symbolic regulator.
    Instructions are first-class syntactic objects:

      RegulatorInstruction
      RegulatorInstructionTag

    Instruction validity is both inductive and Boolean:

      regulator_instruction_valid
      regulator_instruction_valid_bool

    Components and regulated proof scripts are then built from valid
    instructions:

      RegulatorTheoryComponent
      CheckedRegulatorTheoryComponent
      regulator_theory_component_family
      regulator_theory_regulated_suffix
      regulator_theory_regulated_proof

    The key characterization is:

      regulator_theory_regulated_proof R Gamma p
        iff
      proof_script_check_from_bool R Gamma nil p = true

    The package then exposes the abstract symbolic regulator interface:

      SymbolicRegulator
      S_λ
      SymbolicRegulatorWorld

    Here `S_λ` is only a constructive alias for `SymbolicRegulator`. It does
    not import arithmetic coding, arithmetic provability predicates, modal
    axioms, or model theory. Its role is to package a Boolean regulator with
    its derivability predicate and the proof that Boolean acceptance is
    equivalent to derivability.


8 - Regulator Adequacy and Evaluation Closure
    -----------------------------------------

    M001_09__Regulator_Adequacy.v connects the symbolic regulator, machine,
    and closure views:

      regulator_theory_machine
      finite_axiom_set_machine
      regulator_theory_closure
      finite_axiom_set_closure
      regulator_theory_equivalent

    The central bridge lemmas say that closure, checked derivability, and
    symbolic-regulator derivability agree:

      regulator_theory_closure_iff_checked_derivable_lemma
      regulator_theory_closure_iff_symbolic_derivable_lemma
      finite_axiom_set_closure_as_regulator_theory_closure_lemma

    The closure layer also exports modus ponens, deduction, reductio,
    negative precomposition, and monotonicity in closure form.

    M001_10__Evaluation_Closure.v defines generic evaluation frames and their
    regulator-theory version:

      EvaluationFrame
      RegulatorEvaluationFrame

    Evaluation completeness yields a fixed point:

      evaluation_frame_fixed_point_lemma
      regulator_evaluation_frame_fixed_point_lemma

    Fixed-point-free transformers rule out complete frames:

      fixed_point_free_obstructs_frame
      regulator_equiv_fixed_point_free
      regulator_fixed_point_free_obstructs_frame

    This is only an evaluation-closure bridge. L001 interprets it as a
    fixed-point/collapse theorem; M001 itself does not add the obstruction
    principle.


9 - Active Tree
    -----------

    M001_00_Premises
      -> M001_01__Kernel
      -> M001_02__Abstraction
      -> M001_03__Falsity
      -> M001_04__Certificates
      -> M001_05__Application
      -> M001_06__Syntactic_Adequacy
      -> M001_07__Obstruction
      -> M001_08__Symbolic_Regulator
      -> M001_09__Regulator_Adequacy
      -> M001_10__Evaluation_Closure
      -> M001_95_API
      -> M001_97_Artifacts
      -> M001_98_Sanity


10 - Files
     -----

    M001_00_Premises.v

      Canonical definitions for formulas, contexts, axiom sets, regulator
      profiles, regulator theories, Boolean environments, justifications,
      proof lines, and proof scripts.

    M001_01__Kernel.v

      Formula equality, context membership, finite axiom-set membership, K/S
      and optional EFQ recognition, proof-line validation, proof-script
      checking, and finite axiom-set checking.

    M001_02__Abstraction.v

      Executable deduction transformer and checked deduction theorem.

    M001_03__Falsity.v

      Object-language negation, constructive reductio, and reductio
      certificate surfaces.

    M001_04__Certificates.v

      Checked proof certificates and checked derivability vocabulary.

    M001_05__Application.v

      Proof-script shifting, checked modus-ponens composition, and checked
      closure lemmas for assumptions and axioms.

    M001_06__Syntactic_Adequacy.v

      Inductive derivability and checked/inductive adequacy.

    M001_07__Obstruction.v

      Negative transport through checked implications, context monotonicity,
      regulator-theory monotonicity, finite axiom-set monotonicity, and
      antitone non-derivability transport.

    M001_08__Symbolic_Regulator.v

      Regulator instructions, regulator components, regulated proof scripts,
      symbolic regulator records, `S_λ`, finite and abstract regulator
      instances, and checker/regulator equivalence.

    M001_09__Regulator_Adequacy.v

      Machine views, closure views, equivalence inside closure, and closure
      principles for the regulator theory.

    M001_10__Evaluation_Closure.v

      Generic and regulator evaluation frames, fixed-point lemmas, and
      fixed-point-free frame obstruction forms.

    M001_95_API.v

      Public import boundary for the completed M001 stack.

    M001_97_Artifacts.v

      Assumption reports and extraction/artifact boundary. The extracted
      source artifact is `regulator_theory_checker.ml/.mli`.

    M001_98_Sanity.v

      Smoke tests for syntax, checking, proof transformers, certificates,
      adequacy, negative transport, symbolic regulators, closure, and
      evaluation.


11 - Public Import
     -------------

    Downstream developments should normally import:

      From M001 Require Import M001_95_API.

    L001 does exactly this and then builds the Aporetic fixed-point theorem
    over `RegulatorClosure` and `RegulatorClosureEvaluationFrame`.
